[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

How would you like to approach this? Feel free to take a moment to gather your thoughts first.

[candidate silent 50s]

[candidate · 2:20] Here's how I'd frame it. Margin fell from 24% to 6% while revenue grew 15%, so this is almost certainly a cost problem — costs grew materially faster than revenue. I'd want to confirm that first, then isolate where.

Three buckets:

First, revenue quality. The 15% growth — is it from new stores, more transactions per store, or price? Growth from new-store openings can carry fixed costs before they mature, which dilutes margin even if unit economics are fine.

Second, cost structure. I'd split COGS — coffee beans, milk, cups — from store-level operating costs like labor and rent, then corporate overhead. I want each as a percent of revenue, two years ago versus today.

Third, external factors: commodity prices, wage inflation, lease renewals, competition.

To start — do we have the P&L broken into those cost lines for both years?
