[candidate · 4:43] Cost structure, second bucket — and specifically each line as a percent of revenue across the two years.

Reason is arithmetic. Revenue grew 15%. If margin went 24% to 6%, then on a base of 100 going to 115, profit went from 24 to about 6.9 — so absolute profit fell roughly 70% while the business got bigger. Total costs went from 76 to 108, up about 42% against 15% revenue growth. That gap is large enough that it almost has to sit in one or two line items, not be spread evenly.

So the fastest path is a percent-of-revenue bridge: whichever line moved most in basis points is the culprit, and that single step also tells me whether it's a store-level cost or overhead.

The other two buckets I'd use as explanations once I've located the line — new-store dilution and commodity or wage inflation are causes, not locations. No point theorizing about why before I know where.
