[candidate · 14:08] My recommendation: Brew & Bean's margin decline is an input-cost pass-through failure, not an operations problem — the fix is a staged menu price increase paired with bean procurement reform.

Three supports. One, COGS explains 16 of the 18 margin points; labor and overhead are essentially stable. Two, within COGS, beans more than doubled — up ~123% — with other inputs up 37.5%. Three, prices haven't moved in two years, so we ate all of it.

Sizing the lever: current index is revenue 115, costs 108.1, profit 6.9. A 10% price increase, volume held, adds 11.5 straight to profit — 18.4 on 126.5 revenue, a 14.5% margin. Restoring 24% would need roughly 24% pricing, which I wouldn't attempt at once.

Risks: specialty coffee demand elasticity, and competitors who may have already repriced.

Next steps: elasticity test in a subset of the 200 stores, and hedge or lock bean contracts so we're not repricing again in six months.
