[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 14:10] Before recommendations — one open question.

Beyond pricing and procurement, what else could Brew and Bean do to rebuild margin? Give me a structured set of options.

[candidate silent 50s]

[candidate · 17:10] Three categories, beyond price and procurement.

First, product mix. Beans are now roughly 35% of COGS, so shift volume toward items where beans aren't the main input — food, teas, cold brew concentrate served in larger formats. Bundling a lower-bean-content item with coffee raises ticket without touching headline coffee prices, which also protects the elasticity risk I flagged.

Second, portion and waste economics. If beans doubled in cost, every gram of over-pouring or end-of-day dumped brew is now twice as expensive. I'd audit grams per shot, brewed-coffee waste rates, and dairy spoilage. This is a real lever precisely because the input got expensive — the same waste rate costs far more than it did two years ago.

Third, overhead. It drifted 12 to 14 points, and in absolute terms 12 to 16.1 — up 34% against 15% revenue growth. Smaller than COGS, but it's the one line growing faster than the business that nobody's examined. Worth understanding whether that's new-store support costs or corporate creep.

I'd note labor is deliberately absent — flat at 22% means no evident slack, and cutting baristas in a specialty chain risks the service quality that justifies the price increase.
