[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 17:18] Understood. Now give me your final recommendation to the CEO — headline first, in under a minute.

[candidate silent 50s]

[candidate · 20:19] Headline: your margin problem is coffee bean inflation you never passed through to customers. Raise prices and lock your bean supply.

The evidence: of the 18-point margin decline, 16 points sit in COGS. Labor is flat at 22% of revenue, so this isn't an operations or productivity failure. Inside COGS, beans more than doubled — up about 123% — and other inputs rose 37.5%, while menu prices haven't moved in two years. You absorbed the entire input shock.

The fix, in sequence: a staged price increase, starting around 10%, which on current economics adds roughly 11.5 points and takes margin from 6% to about 14.5%. Then hedge or contract beans so you're not repricing again in six months. Then mix shift and portion control to claw back the rest.

Risk is elasticity — test in a subset of stores before rolling to all 200.

I would not cut labor. It's the service quality that lets you charge specialty prices.
